INT. GRAND PALMERSTON HOTEL - VENTRILOQUISTS' CONVENTION - BACKSTAGE - NIGHT

A cramped green room crowded with trunks of wooden dummies. Glass eyes glint from hat boxes. A sign above the door reads: ANNUAL INTERNATIONAL CONGRESS OF VENTRILOQUISTS - 47TH YEAR. Muffled applause drifts through the wall.

MORTIMER PLUMLEY, 50s, a tuxedo and a hairpiece, sits before a mirror, adjusting a dummy on his knee: a wooden schoolboy in a cap, grinning. BEATRICE OKONJO, 40s, in a sequined gown, tunes a puppet duck. HUGO VANDERKELEN, 30s, a nervous newcomer in a rented tux, clutches a lone lamb-shaped sock puppet.

MORTIMER
Quiet, Hugo. You're twitching.

HUGO
I'm not twitching.

BEATRICE
You are twitching.

HUGO
It's a tic.

MORTIMER
A tic is fine. A twitch is a tell.

HUGO
Mortimer, I can't do this.

MORTIMER
Of course you can.

HUGO
My lamb has no personality.

MORTIMER
Then give it one.

HUGO
How?

MORTIMER
(in his dummy's voice, high and cheeky)
Just make it sassy!

The dummy's mouth clacks. Beatrice's duck quacks in agreement. Hugo's lamb droops. A speaker above the door crackles.

MRS. PEMBERTON (V.O.)
(over PA, brisk)
Attention, ventriloquists. The Congress will begin the Grand Finale in ten minutes. Contestants please report to the stage left wing. That includes Mr. Vanderkelen.

HUGO
(squeaking)
She said my name.

BEATRICE
She says everyone's name.

MORTIMER
(as the dummy)
Don't wet yourself, kid.

HUGO
I'm going to be sick.

BEATRICE
Not on the duck.

She turns the duck away. Mortimer's dummy slides off his knee and tumbles across the floor into a pile of trunks. He lunges. Hugo lunges. Beatrice lunges. All three collide, the duck squawking, the lamb flying through the air, wooden limbs rattling.

Mortimer lands on top of Hugo. Beatrice lands on both. The dummy lands upright on a trunk, grinning.

MORTIMER
(muffled)
Get off.

HUGO
(muffled)
I can't.

BEATRICE
Everyone get off me!

MRS. PEMBERTON (V.O.)
(over PA)
Contestants. Eight minutes.

They untangle. Beatrice dusts off her gown. Hugo retrieves the lamb, its ear torn. Mortimer tests the dummy's jaw.

HUGO
Is it broken?

MORTIMER
Cheek's loose.

BEATRICE
Duct tape.

MORTIMER
I don't carry duct tape to a gala.

BEATRICE
I do.

She digs into her purse and produces a roll. Together they bind the dummy's jaw, then the lamb's ear. Hugo is breathing steadier now.

HUGO
It looks ridiculous.

MORTIMER
Ridiculous wins.

BEATRICE
Ridiculous is the whole point.

HUGO
What if I freeze?

MORTIMER
Then freeze.

HUGO
That's your advice?

MORTIMER
Freeze convincingly.

Beatrice laughs. A spotlight flares in the doorway. The applause on the other side of the wall swells.

MRS. PEMBERTON (V.O.)
(over PA)
Five minutes!

HUGO
(squeaking)
Five minutes!

BEATRICE
You'll be fine.

MORTIMER
(as the dummy)
Break a leg, lamb chop.

HUGO
(in a tiny lamb voice, trembling)
Baa.

They all stare at the lamb, which, for the first time, seems to look back.

MORTIMER
There it is.

BEATRICE
That's personality.

HUGO
(lamb voice, firm)
Baa!

He marches to the door, lamb on hand, chin up. Beatrice and Mortimer follow, one with a duck under her arm, one with a taped dummy on his shoulder. The curtain parts. Light and applause flood in. They step through.
